# Install Obot Sentry on macOS

Install Obot Sentry on a Mac without an MDM. This download contains:

- `obot-sentry.pkg` — the installer (version {{.obotSentryVersion}}). Installs
  the agent and schedules scans and hook convergence, exactly as the MDM
  channels do.
- `obot-sentry` — the bare universal binary, for installing by hand instead.

Everything below needs an administrator account.

## Installer (pkg)

1. Write the deployment configuration, replacing `REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY`
   with your enrollment key. This is the same preferences domain an MDM profile
   delivers, in the location macOS reserves for local administrators:

   ```sh
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/ai.obot.obot-sentry ServerURL -string "{{.serverURL}}"
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/ai.obot.obot-sentry EnrollmentKey -string "REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY"
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/ai.obot.obot-sentry ScanIntervalMinutes -int {{.scanIntervalMinutes}}
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/ai.obot.obot-sentry EnforcementEnabled -bool {{if .enforcementEnabled}}true{{else}}false{{end}}
   ```

2. Install the agent:

   ```sh
   sudo installer -pkg obot-sentry.pkg -target /
   ```

   Configuring first means the first scan and hook convergence run immediately,
   because the installer starts both launchd jobs. The reverse order works too —
   the jobs pick the configuration up on their next run.

3. Enroll the device:

   ```sh
   sudo obot-sentry enroll
   ```

   Enroll verifies the configuration and prints the device ID this Mac reports
   as. Scans enroll on their own, so this is a check rather than a
   prerequisite — and it is idempotent, so running it again is harmless.

4. Grant Obot Sentry Full Disk Access in *System Settings > Privacy & Security >
   Full Disk Access*, adding `/usr/local/bin/obot-sentry`. Scans run in the
   background, where macOS cannot prompt for consent, so without this they
   silently skip protected locations such as `~/Desktop`, `~/Documents`, and
   `~/Downloads`.

   The MDM channels push this grant as a profile instead. macOS only honors
   Full Disk Access profiles that arrive from a user-approved MDM, so there is
   no profile you can install by hand to skip this step.

5. Confirm both jobs are loaded:

   ```sh
   launchctl print "gui/$(id -u)/ai.obot.obot-sentry.scan" | head -5
   sudo launchctl print system/ai.obot.obot-sentry.hook-install | head -5
   ```

   The device appears in Obot as its first scan arrives. To watch the agent
   work:

   ```sh
   log stream --predicate 'process == "obot-sentry"'
   ```

## What the installer does

- Installs `obot-sentry` to `/usr/local/bin`.
- Installs a LaunchAgent (`ai.obot.obot-sentry.scan`) that macOS runs in each
  signed-in user's session, as that user, at login and every 15 minutes. Each
  run submits a scan at most every {{.scanIntervalMinutes}} minutes — change
  `ScanIntervalMinutes` in step 1 to retune it without reinstalling.
- Installs a LaunchDaemon (`ai.obot.obot-sentry.hook-install`) that runs as root
  at load and hourly, converging the managed local-agent audit hooks for the
  signed-in user. It reads `EnforcementEnabled` from step 1 on every run, so
  turning enforcement on or off there takes effect without reinstalling.
- Prepares `/Library/Application Support/obot/obot-sentry`, where the device
  identity shared by every user on the Mac is stored.

## Standalone binary (Alternate)

Install `obot-sentry` by hand to run one-off scans or install hooks yourself.
Use this to try Obot Sentry without the automated scanning and hook maintenance
the pkg sets up — nothing is scheduled, so every scan is one you run.

1. Install the binary. `hook-install` writes this exact path into every hook and
   refuses to run if nothing is there, so the location is not optional:

   ```sh
   sudo install -m 755 obot-sentry /usr/local/bin/obot-sentry
   ```

2. Configure Obot Sentry, replacing `REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY` with your
   enrollment key:

   ```sh
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/ai.obot.obot-sentry ServerURL -string "{{.serverURL}}"
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/ai.obot.obot-sentry EnrollmentKey -string "REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY"
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/ai.obot.obot-sentry ScanIntervalMinutes -int {{.scanIntervalMinutes}}
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/ai.obot.obot-sentry EnforcementEnabled -bool {{if .enforcementEnabled}}true{{else}}false{{end}}
   ```

3. Enroll the device:

   ```sh
   sudo obot-sentry enroll
   ```

   Enroll verifies the configuration and prints the device ID this Mac reports
   as.

### Run a scan

```sh
obot-sentry scan --submit --force
```

Scans are submitted at most once every {{.scanIntervalMinutes}} minutes, and a run within that window skips submission. `--force` bypasses the interval, so a scan you run by hand always submits. The same two commands work after a pkg install, where the launchd jobs also run them on their own.

To scan a Mac without installing anything at all, run the downloaded binary with
the configuration in its environment — this needs no root and writes nothing:

```sh
OBOT_SENTRY_SERVER_URL="{{.serverURL}}" \
  OBOT_SENTRY_ENROLLMENT_KEY="REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY" \
  ./obot-sentry scan --submit
```

### Install hooks

```sh
sudo obot-sentry hook-install{{if .enforcementEnabled}} --enforce{{end}}
```

Hooks are installed once and stay as written. Without the pkg's LaunchDaemon
nothing re-checks them, so rerun this after a change to bring them back.

{{if .enforcementEnabled}}
## Tool-call enforcement

Each tool call in Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor is checked against Obot's
allowlist before it runs. The check is a single request to Obot, and it is
**fail-closed**: if Obot cannot be reached, the device is not enrolled, or the
hook cannot tell which MCP server a call targets, the call is blocked.

- Blocking a call never *approves* one. Your agents' own approval prompts still
  apply to everything that is allowed.
- Visual Studio Code is not supported for enforcement. Its audit hook is unaffected.
- An MCP server can be allowlisted by URL, by its `npx`/`uvx` package, or — for a
  claude.ai connector — by display name. A stdio server started from a local
  executable path presents none of those, so it cannot be allowlisted and is
  blocked by design.

Turning enforcement off in Obot stops the blocking immediately — the server
allows every call when enforcement is disabled — even before the hooks
themselves are removed.
{{end}}
## Uninstall

Uninstall in this order: remove the hooks first, then the agent. Step 2 runs
`hook-uninstall` before it removes anything, so the hooks go either way.

### 1. Remove the hooks

Remove every Obot Sentry-managed hook while preserving third-party hooks:

```sh
sudo obot-sentry hook-uninstall
```

The command removes only entries marked `--managed-by obot-sentry`; it does not
delete files or change unmarked supporting settings. If desired, manually remove
the Codex `[features]` pins `hooks = true` and
`non_prefixed_mcp_tool_names = false`, and remove the Obot-added values under VS
Code's `chat.hookFilesLocations`.

The per-user files (`~/.claude`, `~/.copilot`, VS Code settings) exist once per
signed-in user, so repeat for each user on a shared Mac. Restart Claude Code,
Codex, VS Code, and Cursor afterward to drop the hooks.

### 2. Remove the agent

`uninstall.sh` runs `hook-uninstall` first, then unloads both launchd jobs and
removes the binary, the plists, the package receipt, the configuration, the
shared device identity, and every user's scan state. It covers either install
path — the parts a standalone install never created are skipped:

```sh
sudo bash uninstall.sh
```

Then remove `/usr/local/bin/obot-sentry` from Full Disk Access in System
Settings — the grant you added by hand is the one thing the script cannot
revoke.
